CLC_OPEN_WOUNDS, specifically.  The high priest's line in monst.c is:

2510    A(ATTK(AT_WEAP, AD_PHYS, 4,10), ATTK(AT_KICK, AD_PHYS, 2, 8),
2511      ATTK(AT_MAGC, AD_CLRC, 2, 8), ATTK(AT_MAGC, AD_CLRC, 2, 8),

To calculate the damage, we go to mcastu.c:

 300        dmg = d((int)((ml/2) + mattk->damn), (int)mattk->damd);

A quick wizmode check says that the high priest is level 30; following
the above formula, his open wounds will be 17d8, which works out to
anywhere between 17 and 136.  Half spell damage will, of course, 
reduce this by half and is nice to have.  But wait -- there's more;

832     case CLC_OPEN_WOUNDS:
833    if (Antimagic) {
834        shieldeff(u.ux, u.uy);
836        dmg = (dmg + 1) / 2;
837    }

So here we see that magic resistance also reduces the damage by half,
meaning that the max damage for this spell to a character wearing magic
resistance is actually 68.  The high priest does get two AD_CLRC attacks,
but this is mostly meaningless since the timeout for monster spellcasting
ensures that he only ever gets to use one at a time.

If you were "killed quite instantly" by this spell, you were running
around with some very shoddy HP _and_ got some very bad luck.  I do note
the qualifier "AFAIR", though, and I think perhaps referring to
'selective memory' is appropriate at this time.

